# Line endings are DECLARED here, not inherited from whoever checked the repo out.
#
# ⛔ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Until 0.0.20 there was no `.gitattributes` at all, so a Windows
# checkout took Git for Windows' `core.autocrlf=true` default and every text file arrived with
# CRLF. `bash` then reads `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and a line-anchored `grep`/`sed` inside a
# shell script stops matching the last field on every line. That is not a cosmetic difference:
# it made mokata's own shell tooling fail on the platform, and the failures did not look like
# line endings — they looked like the tools lying about the repo.
#
# MEASURED at the 0.0.20 cut, by converting a mirror checkout to CRLF on Linux and re-running:
#   * `test_floor_provisioner` — `floor-python.sh` reported "pyproject.toml declares no
#     `requires-python`" against a pyproject.toml that declares exactly that.
#   * `test_a9_mutant_batches_are_swept` — the sweep reported "0 mutants swept", i.e. a corpus
#     that reaches nothing reports "no stale patterns" for the same reason a working one does.
# Both reproduce from CRLF alone, with no Windows involved, and both were red on the mirror's
# windows-latest leg.
#
# ⭐ LF IS THE DECLARATION, and the two exceptions are declarations too. A `.cmd`/`.bat` is read
# by cmd.exe, which is the one consumer here that genuinely wants CRLF, so it is named rather
# than left to a default — the same rule the rest of this repo applies to pragmas and pins.
* text=auto eol=lf

# cmd.exe is the exception, and it is stated rather than inherited.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.whl binary
